{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2138-10-30 18:45:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hello, Mr. ___. I'm Dr. ___, one of your hospital doctors. You're here for monitoring after your bronchoscopy and airway dilation today. How are you feeling?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2138-10-30 18:46:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Hi, doctor. I’m okay. My breathing feels normal, like my usual baseline. But my trach site is a bit sore."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2138-10-30 18:47:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That makes sense. You had dilation of both your left and right mainstem bronchi today, which can cause some localized discomfort. There was just a little oozing, and we gave you some medicine to prevent swelling."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2138-10-30 18:48:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Right, I get these every four weeks. But I’m glad the procedure went smoothly. Last time, the trach got displaced and I ended up in the ICU."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2138-10-30 18:48:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, I reviewed your history. That was a scary moment, but you recovered well. This time, your trach is secure, and your oxygen levels are excellent—99% on room air."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2138-10-30 18:49:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Good to hear. Should I be worried about breathing problems this time?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2138-10-30 18:50:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not at this point. Your lungs are moving well, and we’ll keep monitoring you. We did a chest X-ray after the procedure to make sure there’s no air leak or collapse."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2138-10-30 18:51:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And what did it show?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2138-10-30 18:51:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The lung volumes are a bit low, which is common after sedation, but no pneumothorax. There’s some stable atelectasis on the right side, which we’ve seen before. Nothing new or concerning."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2138-10-30 18:52:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, thanks. My chest pain is mostly at the trach site, not deep in the lungs."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2138-10-30 18:53:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s consistent with post-procedure soreness. We’ll manage that with pain medication. You’re on acetaminophen and ibuprofen regularly, and oxycodone if needed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2138-10-30 18:54:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’d prefer to avoid the oxycodone if I can. I’ve used it before, but I don’t like how it makes me feel."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2138-10-30 18:54:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s completely fine. We’ll stick with Tylenol and ibuprofen first. Let us know if the pain gets worse, especially with breathing or swallowing."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2138-10-31 07:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. I just reviewed your labs from this morning. Your white blood cell count was a bit high last night—14.2—but it’s come down to 7.6 today."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2138-10-31 07:16:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Was that from infection?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2138-10-31 07:17:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not necessarily. It’s common after a procedure like this. The inflammation from the dilation can raise the count temporarily. With no fever or other signs, we don’t think it’s an infection."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2138-10-31 07:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s a relief. My stomach’s been a little off too—could that be from the Esbriet?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2138-10-31 07:18:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, your team has noted that your GERD symptoms may be related to Esbriet. We’re keeping you on both famotidine and pantoprazole to protect your stomach."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2138-10-31 07:19:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Good. I’ll keep taking those. How long will I need to stay this time?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2138-10-31 07:20:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Usually, patients like you stay 2–3 days for pain control after dilation. Since you’re doing well, we’re hoping to discharge you day after tomorrow."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2138-10-31 07:21:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That sounds good. I have another dilation scheduled in four weeks, right?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2138-10-31 07:21:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, and there’s also a plan to place a custom trach at that time. We’ll coordinate that with your ENT and pulmonology teams."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2138-10-31 14:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Just checking in. How’s the pain today?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2138-10-31 14:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Better. The ibuprofen helps. I only took one dose of oxycodone last night."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2138-10-31 14:01:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Excellent. Your breathing sounds are improving too—just faint wheezes now, no rales. We’ll keep encouraging deep breathing and coughing to keep your lungs clear."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2138-10-31 14:02:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve been doing the incentive spirometer every hour like they showed me."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2138-10-31 14:03:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Perfect. That helps prevent atelectasis, especially with your history. Your blood pressure is stable too—110 over 65 this morning."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2138-10-31 14:04:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve been taking my losartan every day. Should I adjust the dose?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2138-10-31 14:04:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "No, stay on your usual dose. We’re monitoring it, and it’s well-controlled. We’ll keep you on all your home meds except adjusting pain management as needed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2138-11-01 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good news—your labs this morning are stable. Your bicarbonate is a bit high at 30, but that’s likely from mild chronic respiratory retention, not new."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2138-11-01 08:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is that related to my lung condition?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2138-11-01 08:01:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, from long-standing airway disease and fibrosis. But your oxygen levels are strong, so we’re not concerned. Your kidney and liver functions are normal too."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2138-11-01 08:02:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my breathing? Still okay?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2138-11-01 08:03:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, you’re breathing comfortably at rest, rate of 18, saturating 99% on trach collar. We’ll do a final check tomorrow, and if all stays well, you can go home in the afternoon."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2138-11-01 08:04:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Great. I’ll need to refill the pain meds, though."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2138-11-01 08:04:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll send prescriptions for acetaminophen and ibuprofen—both 10 days’ supply. The oxycodone is still available if needed, but we encourage using it sparingly."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2138-11-01 08:05:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Understood. I’ll follow up with my pulmonologist after the next procedure."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2138-11-02 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Morning! You’re looking well. Final exam shows your trach site is clean, no bleeding. Lungs are clear with just a little wheeze. We’re clearing you for discharge."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2138-11-02 09:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s great. Any instructions before I go?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2138-11-02 09:01:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Keep taking all your regular medications, including Esbriet, inhalers, and blood pressure pill. Use the pain meds as scheduled for the next few days. Watch for fever, increased pain, or breathing trouble."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2138-11-02 09:02:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And if the trach ever comes out?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2138-11-02 09:03:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Call 911 or go to the ER immediately. But your trach is stable now, and we’ve reinforced the ties. You’ve been through this before—you know the signs."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2138-11-02 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, I do. Thanks for taking good care of me."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2138-11-02 09:04:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. We’ll see you in clinic soon. Take care, and don’t hesitate to call if anything changes."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2138-10-30 18:45:00",
    "admission_end": "2138-11-02 15:22:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "Mr. ___ was admitted for post-procedural monitoring following an elective bronchoscopy with balloon dilation of the left and right mainstem bronchi due to recurrent tracheal stenosis from Ruvalcaba-Myhre-Smith syndrome and relapsing polychondritis. The procedure was performed without immediate complications, and he remained stable throughout the admission with no respiratory distress. His primary complaint was mild pain at the trach site, which was managed with acetaminophen, ibuprofen, and occasional oxycodone. Labs showed an initial elevated white count that normalized, and imaging confirmed no pneumothorax with stable atelectasis. He improved with supportive care, maintained oxygenation on trach collar, and was discharged home on day three with plans for repeat bronchoscopy and custom trach placement in four weeks.",
    "problems": [
      "Other specified diseases of upper respiratory tract",
      "Acute bronchitis, unspecified",
      "Unspecified chronic bronchitis",
      "Pulmonary fibrosis, unspecified",
      "Genetic carrier of other disease",
      "Other diseases of bronchus, not elsewhere classified",
      "Relapsing polychondritis",
      "Gastro-esophageal reflux disease without esophagitis",
      "Essential (primary) hypertension",
      "Encounter for attention to tracheostomy",
      "Other acute postprocedural pain",
      "Other chest pain"
    ]
  }
}
